\begin{abstract}
In the acquired natural Cayley group $G_2(11)$, a subgroup containing a
subgroup $P$ of order seven, an involution $b$ inverting $P$, and a Klein
four group must contain a particular subgroup of order $42$ determined
by $P$ and $b$. This extends the retained argument which required an
additional element of order $19$ in each end. Consequently a rank four
string marking whose middle product has order divisible by seven fails
the intersection property. The proof uses the previously acquired
maximal subgroup classifications, the retained torus normalizer argument,
and two explicit subgroup tables. It evaluates no current marking.
\end{abstract}

\section{Scope and the common completion principle}

Throughout, $G=\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb F_{11}}(\mathcal C)=G_2(11)$ is
the actual full automorphism group of the split Cayley algebra, acting
faithfully on its natural pure seven-space. These are the retained
natural-model and full-image hypotheses, rather than conclusions from
the appearance of a seven-dimensional matrix representation. All
applications must retain their original member, frame and transport joins.

For involutions $a,b,c,e$, put
\[
 L=\gen{a,b,c},\qquad R=\gen{b,c,e},\qquad
 D=\gen{b,c},\qquad Y=bc.
\]
Assume that $a,c$ are distinct nonidentity commuting involutions, and
likewise $b,e$. Thus the two ends contain genuine Klein four groups.
The relation $ae=ea$, required by the full string marking, is not needed
for the obstruction below.

\begin{lemma}[Common completion]
Let $D\leq L\cap R$ be subgroups of a finite group. If a subgroup $B$ is
contained in both ends and $|B|$ does not divide $|D|$, then
$L\cap R\ne D$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Equality would imply $B\leq D$, contradicting Lagrange's theorem.
\end{proof}

For comparison, if the two ends generate the ambient group and are
proper, they cannot both lie in one proper maximal subgroup. Hence a
subgroup of $D$ with a unique maximal overgroup gives a nongeneration
obstruction. No characteristic-subgroup hypothesis is needed for this
fixed-subgroup statement. In $G_2(11)$ this applies to the retained
order-$37$ line theorem. It does not apply directly to order $19$:
both $\SL_3(11):2$ and $J_1$ occur in the complete list. A common literal
completion, as above, handles that situation without confusing
nongeneration with failure of the intersection property.

\section{The retained classification inputs}

The complete maximal-type list of Kleidman, as retained in the
pre-exclusion list of Nguyen and in Wilson, leaves the following types
when a proper maximal subgroup contains an element of order seven:
\[
 \SL_3(11):2,\qquad J_1,\qquad
 2^3.\PSL_3(2),\qquad G_2(2)\cong\PSU_3(3):2.
\tag{1}
\]
The extension in the third entry need not split. The other applicable
entries in the retained complete list have no factor seven in their orders.
The exclusion of $\PSL_2(8)$ is essential here: it has a factor seven.
Burness--Thomas\cite[Proposition~7.4(a,b)]{BT} restrict this exceptional
prime-field case to $p\equiv1,8\pmod 9$; $11\equiv2\pmod 9$ excludes it.
These congruences were explicitly checked in the retained complete-list
review. There is no proper subfield at the prime field $11$; the other
exceptional types retain their stated exclusions.

Three further inputs are used, with their original attribution retained.

