\section{The canonical subgroup over $P$ and $b$}

Let $P\leq G$ have order seven. The retained natural-module argument
gives a unique rational invariant line $\ell$ for $P$. Briefly,
$\operatorname{ord}_7(11)=3$, and the two nontrivial cubic constituents
are dual and distinct. The nondegenerate symmetric form pairs these
constituents and leaves exactly one nondegenerate trivial line. Since
$7\nmid 10$, any rational invariant line is fixed pointwise. Its full
Cayley line stabilizer is of split or unitary type. The latter has order
$2\cdot11^3(11^3+1)(11^2-1)$, which is not divisible by seven.
It is therefore the split $\SL_3(11):2$.

Write $F$ for this stabilizer's $\SL_3(11)$ point kernel. Identifying
its irreducible three-space with $\mathbb F_{11^3}$ gives the retained
centralizer and normalizer calculation
\[
 T=\ker\bigl(\operatorname{Norm}_{\mathbb F_{11^3}/\mathbb F_{11}}\bigr)
       \cong C_{133},\qquad
 \N_F(P)=T:C_3,
\]
\[
 N:=\N_G(P)=T:(C_3\times C_2)\cong C_{133}:C_6.
\tag{4}
\]
The order three action is exponent $11$ on $T$, and the graph
involution acts by inversion. The graph involution is adjoint-inverse
for the trace pairing; it commutes with the Frobenius map. Uniqueness
of $\ell$ rules out a normalizer outside this full line stabilizer.
These are exactly the retained field and trace arguments, with the
order seven subgroup of $T$ in place of its order nineteen subgroup.

The six actions on $P$ are the distinct exponents $\pm11^i$, for
$0\leq i<3$. Consequently
\[
 \C_G(P)=T.
\tag{5}
\]
Suppose an actual nonidentity involution $b$ inverts $P$. Its image
in $N/T$ is the graph involution: among the six displayed exponents,
only that element acts as inversion on $P$. Therefore $b$ inverts all
of $T$. The odd cyclic torus has a bijective square map, so $b$ is
$T$-conjugate to the displayed graph involution. It follows that
\[
 Q:=\C_N(b)\cong C_6,\qquad B:=PQ,\qquad |B|=42.
\tag{6}
\]
Here $Q$ meets $P$ trivially and normalizes it, and $b\in Q$.

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness of the completion]
There is exactly one subgroup of $N$ of order $42$ containing $P$ and
$b$, namely $B$ in (6).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
In the quotient by $P$,
\[
 N/P\cong C_{19}:C_6.
\]
The action of $C_6$ on $C_{19}$ is faithful: exponent $-11$ has order
six modulo $19$. The involution $bP$ inverts the normal $C_{19}$, so
its centralizer in $N/P$ is a cyclic complement of order six. One may
see this by conjugating $bP$ into the displayed complement; the
inversion action has no nonidentity fixed point on the odd kernel.

If $A\leq N$ has order $42$ and contains $P,b$, its image $A/P$ has
order six and meets the normal $C_{19}$ trivially. Projection to the
cyclic quotient $C_6$ is an isomorphism on $A/P$. Thus $A/P$ is abelian
and centralizes $bP$. It is therefore the entire order six centralizer
of $bP$. Its full inverse image has order $42$ and is $A$. This proves
uniqueness. Existence is supplied by $B=PQ$ in (6).
\end{proof}

\section{Why every admissible end contains the completion}

We first isolate the quotient argument needed for the monomial cases.

\begin{lemma}[An odd normalizer in a quotient excludes the reflection]
Let $U$ be a normal $2$-subgroup of a finite group $M$, and let $P\leq M$
have odd prime order. If the normalizer of the image of $P$ in $M/U$
has odd order, then no involution of $M$ inverts $P$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The map on $P$ is injective. If an involution $b$ inverted $P$, its
image would be an element of order at most two in the stated odd
normalizer, hence would be the identity. Thus $b\in U$. For $p\in P$,
the commutator $[b,p]$ belongs to $U$ by normality and to $P$ because
$b$ normalizes $P$. Since $U\cap P=1$, it is trivial. But an inversion
which is also trivial on a nontrivial odd-order group is impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation over a reflected subgroup of order seven]
Let $P\leq G$ have order seven and let an involution $b$ invert $P$.
Every subgroup $H\leq G$ containing $P,b$ and a Klein four group
contains the same subgroup $B$ in (6).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $H=G$ there is nothing to prove. Otherwise place $H$ in a maximal
subgroup from (1).

Suppose first that this maximal subgroup is $2^3.\PSL_3(2)$.
Modulo its normal $2^3$, the subgroup $P$ has order seven. Since
$\PSL_3(2)\cong\PSL_2(7)$ has odd normalizer $7:3$, the preceding
lemma excludes the actual reflection $b$. This case cannot occur.
The argument uses the quotient and does not assume the extension splits.

Next suppose that $H\leq J\cong J_1$. If $H<J$, choose a maximal
overgroup of $H$ in $J$. In (3), only two types have seven. The
$2^3:7:3$ type is excluded by the quotient lemma, since its quotient
$7:3$ is odd. The $7:6$ type has only one factor two in its order,
and therefore cannot contain the Klein four group in $H$. Hence
$H=J$. Its normalizer $\N_H(P)$ has order $42$, contains $P,b$, and
is a subgroup of the actual $N=\N_G(P)$. The uniqueness lemma gives
$\N_H(P)=B$.

Suppose now that $H\leq M=\SL_3(11):2$. Its natural invariant line
is $P$-invariant, so is the unique $\ell$ above; this is the same full
stabilizer as in (4). Set $H_0=H\cap F$, where $F=\SL_3(11)$.
Then $P\leq H_0$. The Klein four group maps to $M/F=C_2$ with a
kernel of order at least two, so $H_0$ also contains a nonidentity
involution. If $H_0<F$, the linear classification leaves either an
odd subgroup of order $399$ or a $\PSL_2(7)$ overgroup. The first is
impossible. Within the second, the only proper overgroups of seven
are odd, so $H_0$ is that entire $\PSL_2(7)$.

The reflection $b$ cannot belong to $F$, because it normalizes $P$
and $\N_F(P)$ has odd order $399$. If $H_0=F$, then $H=M$, so
$N\leq H$ and in particular $B\leq H$. In this branch
$|\N_H(P)|=798$, not $42$. If $H_0\cong\PSL_2(7)$, then
$H=H_0\gen b$, and $b$ normalizes $P$. Thus
\[
 \N_H(P)=\N_{H_0}(P)\ \dot\cup\ \N_{H_0}(P)b,
 \qquad |\N_H(P)|=2\cdot21=42.
\]
Again this literal normalizer is $B$ by uniqueness.

Finally suppose that $H\leq M=G_2(2)=K:2$, with
$K=\PSU_3(3)$. Set $H_0=H\cap K$. As before, $H_0$ contains $P$
and an involution. If $H_0<K$, the retained complete $K$ table puts
$H_0$ in $\PSL_2(7)$. Its only proper seven-overgroups are odd, so
$H_0$ is that whole $\PSL_2(7)$. Therefore $H_0$ is either $K$ or
$\PSL_2(7)$. In both alternatives its $P$-normalizer has order $21$.
In particular $b\notin K$, since $\N_K(P)$ is odd. Hence
$H=H_0\gen b$, and the same two-coset argument gives
$|\N_H(P)|=42$. The uniqueness lemma identifies it with $B$.

Every possible maximal overgroup has now been treated. In every
surviving case $B\leq H$, as required.
\end{proof}

\section{The middle product and the application interface}

\begin{corollary}
Under the original marking hypotheses in Section 1, if the order of
$Y=bc$ is divisible by seven, then $L\cap R\ne D$. In particular the
four marks fail the rank four intersection property.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Let $P$ be the unique order seven subgroup of the cyclic group
$\gen Y$. The involution $b$ inverts $Y$ and hence inverts $P$.
The element $Y$ centralizes $P$, so (5) gives $Y\in T\cong C_{133}$.
Its order is therefore seven or $133$. The two distinct involutions
$b,c$ generate the dihedral group of order $14$ or $266$ respectively.

Apply the theorem to each end, using $\gen{a,c}$ on the left and
$\gen{b,e}$ on the right as its Klein four group. Both ends contain
the same $B$ of order $42$. But $3\nmid |D|$. The common completion
lemma gives $L\cap R\ne D$.
\end{proof}

The smallest new actual certificate is
\[
 Y=bc\ne I,\qquad Y^7=I.
\tag{7}
\]
No end word of order $19$, moved line, subgroup closure, invariant-line
search or explicit matrix for $B$ is a further hypothesis. In the
retained direct $19$ caller, $Y^7$ is already computed. A separately
reviewed successor could use $Y^7=I$ and $Y\ne I$ as a distinct new
branch. The existing caller does not implement this branch, and its
minimal failed records do not contain a certified middle matrix from
which new case credit may be imported. Every actual new adoption still
requires its original-context join and fresh owned verification.

Alternatively $Z=Y^{19}\ne I$, $Z^7=I$ is a sufficient predicate for
the same corollary, since it implies that $|Y|$ is seven or $133$.
The latter order is already covered by the retained $19$ branch.
After the existing $19$ and $37$ criteria, (7) is the genuinely additional
middle-order family. No distribution or count of current residual
markings has been evaluated here.

The order-$11$ one-Jordan-block theorem remains available with its two
actual end-$19$ witnesses. The present proof does not remove those
hypotheses, does not settle the possible even or mixed middle orders,
and does not presume that all residual markings must be rejected.
The remaining middle orders require separate arguments. Completing
the census still requires every original predicate and the original
equivalence and accounting obligations.

